    "stop_at_k": 2,
    "stopping_rule_result": "The acceptance says to add planes until marginal gain per physical cost falls below another mechanism, and the ladder answers with a number. The second plane buys 42.7% error reduction for 9.8% more ALU: 4.36 points of error per point of ALU. The third buys 28.1% for 50.3%: 0.56 points per point, an order of magnitude collapse, and it carries the codec from 17.9% UNDER the decode budget to 23.5% OVER it while costing more ALU than flat q3 for only 9.8% less error. The ladder stops at TWO planes.",
    "no_domination": "Function-fitting is a large real improvement to the family but does NOT produce a candidate that dominates the incumbent on the three-axis vector. k2 is 23% fewer bits and 22% less ALU than flat q3 but 25.4% MORE functional error. k3 is 9.8% less error but 15% more bits and 17.2% more ALU. Neither dominates, and saying so is the result -- the prime directive forbids letting a bits win overrule the executable.",
    "incidental": "flat q4 sits at 0.8046 ps/element, BELOW the 0.8092 budget and below q3's 0.8524, while carrying 0.426x q3's error. The extra bit is free in ALU and buys 2.3x the fidelity, which is the same inversion already recorded for this pair."
